# Keep secrets on every Wrangler deploy

Production already lost Cloudflare secrets once. They are not in `.dev.vars` or git. Do not ask the user to paste them, and do not create a local copy.

## Deploy command

When the user asks for a Worker deploy, this is the whole command:

```bash
npx wrangler deploy --env production --keep-vars
```

Use `--env staging` for staging. Pass `--config` only for `workers/tav-intelligence-worker` or `workers/maxbuy-worker`.

`--keep-vars` is required every time. Top-level `keep_vars = true` does not apply to `--env production` or `--env staging`. Wrangler 4.90 ignores `keep_vars` inside an environment, so the flag is the setting that keeps dashboard text vars. Do not put `keep_vars` under `[env.*]`.

## Never on a deploy

- `wrangler secret list`, `put`, `delete`, or `bulk`
- `wrangler versions deploy`, `rollback`, or `wrangler delete`
- Reading secrets through any API or MCP, including secret names
- Opening, grepping, or printing `.dev.vars` or `.env*`
- A before/after secret list. That touches secrets. Skip it.

A deploy with `--keep-vars` uploads code and the plain `[vars]` already in `wrangler.toml`. Secrets stay on the Worker.

## Do not

- Remove `keep_vars = true` from the top level or from an environment
- Add a `[vars]` name that matches a secret
- Change `name` or `[env.*.name]` (that creates a Worker with no secrets)
- Deploy without `--env` (that is a different Worker)

Fly deploys of `tav-seller-enrich` are allowed. Still never print a secret value.
